==Old English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|gem-pro|ang}} {{term/t|gem-pro|*kattaz}}. Cognate with {{etyl|osx|-}} {{term|katto|lang=osx}}, {{etyl|non|-}} {{term|kǫttr|lang=non}} ({{etyl|sv|-}} {{term|katt|lang=sv}}), {{etyl|goh|-}} {{term|kazzo|lang=goh}}.  The word existed in the Germanic languages in a female gender also, represented in Old English by {{term|catte|lang=ang}}.  The word appears to be related to {{etyl|LL.|-}} {{term|cattus|lang=la}} as well as to similar words in the Slavic and Celtic languages, but the ultimate source is uncertain.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈkatt/|lang=ang}}

